he change of measure in the action induced by the transformation t → τ, and both ρ and ξ in this expression must be thought of as functions of the parameter τ. In general, the new time variable τ(t) may be considered as a nonlinear function in t. In particular, we can redeﬁne the time variable τ(t) such that\n\n1 m(t)ρ2(t)\n\n. (29) This transformation implies that the coeﬃcient of the kinetic energy term in equation\n\nτ˙ =\n\n![](<2503.06384_pg7_images/imageFile13.png>)\n\n(28) becomes constant. Consequently, we obtain\n\n  dξ\n\n  . (30)\n\n2\n\ndξ dτ\n\n- 1\n\n![](<2503.06384_pg7_images/imageFile14.png>)\n\n- 2\n\n\n− Ω2ξ2\n\nL ξ,\n\n,τ =\n\n![](<2503.06384_pg7_images/imageFile15.png>)\n\n![](<2503.06384_pg7_images/imageFile16.png>)\n\ndτ\n\nThis Lagrangian describes the motion of a simple harmonic oscillator with constant frequency Ω. The Euler-Lagrange equation for the dynamical variable ξ(τ) is given by\n\nd2ξ dτ2\n\n+ Ω2ξ = 0 . (31)\n\n![](<2503.06384_pg7_images/imageFile17.png>)\n\n",
